# 30. Digests and encodings

A **digest** is the fixed-size output of a cryptographic hash function over a
sequence of octets: equal input gives an equal digest, and a change to any
octet changes it. An **encoding** writes octets as text-safe characters and
reads them back. This chapter covers the two modules that provide them,
`crypto.digest` and `encoding`, over a `String`, a
[`Bytes`](02-data-types/bytes.hmd) value and a `Data` tree.

## The digest module

`crypto.digest` is not in the prelude. A program writes
`import crypto.digest` and reaches the module as `digest`; without the
import, the name is a check error:

```hql
"abc".digest(Sha256)
```

```text
check error[unknown-name]: `digest` is declared in the module `crypto.digest`: write `import crypto.digest`
 --> <expression>:1:1
  |
1 | "abc".digest(Sha256)
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

An algorithm is named by a type, as a format is: `Md5`, `Sha1`, `Sha256`,
`Sha512` and `Blake3`. `s.digest(A)` is the digest of a text's UTF-8 octets
under `A`, written as lowercase hexadecimal, two digits per octet:

```hql
import crypto.digest
s = "abc"
[s.digest(Md5), s.digest(Sha1), s.digest(Sha256), s.digest(Blake3)]
```

```text
["900150983cd24fb0d6963f7d28e17f72", "a9993e364706816aba3e25717850c26c9cd0d89d", "ba7816bf8f01cfea414140de5dae2223b00361a396177a9cb410ff61f20015ad", "6437b3ac38465133ffb63b75273a8db548c558465d79db03fd359c6cd5bd9d85"]
```

The length of the text follows from the algorithm: 32 hex digits for `Md5`,
40 for `Sha1`, 64 for `Sha256` and `Blake3`, 128 for `Sha512`. `Md5` and
`Sha1` are broken for collision resistance: two different inputs with one
digest can be constructed. They serve to compare with digests other tools
wrote, not to protect anything new.

`b.digest(A)` takes a `Bytes` value, the form for octets that are not text.
The two forms agree: the digest of `Bytes(s)` is the digest of `s`:

```hql
import crypto.digest
import encoding
Bytes("68656c6c6f", Hex).digest(Sha256) == "hello".digest(Sha256)
```

```text
true
```

## Fingerprints of data

`fingerprint(x)` is the SHA-256 digest of a `Data` tree's **canonical
JSON**, the text `canonical(x)` gives. Canonical JSON follows RFC 8785:
keys sorted, no whitespace, a number as its digits without trailing zeros,
a time value as its ISO 8601 text and a `Bytes` leaf as its base64 text:

```hql
import crypto.digest
canonical({title: "Dune", pages: 412, price: 9.50, on: Date("2026-10-24")})
```

```text
"{\"on\":\"2026-10-24\",\"pages\":412,\"price\":9.5,\"title\":\"Dune\"}"
```

Two trees equal under HQL equality have one canonical text, so one
fingerprint. Key order, whitespace and `1.10` against `1.1` in the source
text change neither:

```hql
import crypto.digest
a = Data("{\"b\": 1, \"a\": [1.10, \"x\"]}", Json)
b = Data("{ \"a\" : [1.1, \"x\"],\n  \"b\": 1.0 }", Json)
[fingerprint(a), fingerprint(b)]
```

```text
["5b07d3c10bbccec19fd995c80fb3393b8fd39691dbcc412ac55c0d08262ed5af", "5b07d3c10bbccec19fd995c80fb3393b8fd39691dbcc412ac55c0d08262ed5af"]
```

A value's type does count: the number `9.50` and the text `"9.50"` are
different leaves, with different fingerprints:

```hql
import crypto.digest
[fingerprint({price: 9.50}) == fingerprint({price: 9.5}), fingerprint({price: 9.50}) == fingerprint({price: "9.50"})]
```

```text
[true, false]
```

`fingerprint(x)` is `canonical(x).digest(Sha256)`, so a program in another
language reproduces it with any RFC 8785 implementation and SHA-256.

## The encoding module

`encoding` is not in the prelude either: a program writes `import encoding`.
An encoding is named by a type:

| Encoding | Writes octets as |
| --- | --- |
| `Hex` | two hexadecimal digits each, lowercase; either case reads |
| `Base64` | RFC 4648 base64, standard alphabet, padded |
| `Base64Url` | RFC 4648 base64, URL-safe alphabet, unpadded; padding optional on reading |
| `Percent` | RFC 3986 percent-encoding: `A-Z a-z 0-9 - . _ ~` as they are, every other octet as `%XX` |

`s.encode(E)` writes a text's UTF-8 octets in `E`:

```hql
import encoding
["hé".encode(Hex), "??>".encode(Base64), "??>".encode(Base64Url), "a b/é~".encode(Percent)]
```

```text
["68c3a9", "Pz8+", "Pz8-", "a%20b%2F%C3%A9~"]
```

`s.decode(E)` reads the text back as an `Option<String>`. It is `none` where
the input is not valid in `E`, and also where the octets it holds are not
UTF-8, as `/w==` in base64 is the single octet `0xff`:

```hql
import encoding
["68C3A9".decode(Hex), "aGk/".decode(Base64), "6g".decode(Hex), "/w==".decode(Base64)]
```

```text
["hé", "hi?", none, none]
```

Percent-decoding reads `%20` as a space and leaves `+` as it is:

```hql
import encoding
"a+b%20%C3%A9".decode(Percent)
```

```text
"a+b é"
```

### Between text and Bytes

Where the octets are not text, `Bytes` holds them. `b.encode(E)` writes a
`Bytes` value in `E`, and `s.to_bytes(E)` reads it back as an
`Option<Bytes>`, with no UTF-8 requirement; `Bytes(s, E)` reads it as a
value and fails on invalid text. The page [[02-data-types/bytes|Bytes]]
shows each:

```hql
import encoding
b = Bytes("ff00", Hex)
[b.encode(Hex), b.encode(Base64), b.encode(Base64Url)]
```

```text
["ff00", "/wA=", "_wA"]
```

```hql
import encoding
["/w==".decode(Base64), "/w==".to_bytes(Base64)]
```

```text
[none, 0xff]
```

## In practice

### A record already seen

A fingerprint is a key that holds outside the program: in a column, a file
name, a cache. Records that arrive with their keys in another order, or as
another program wrote them, still match the keys of an earlier load. Here
`loaded` holds a 12-digit prefix of each fingerprint an earlier run stored,
and only the new record passes:

```hql
import crypto.digest
loaded = {"106815777291"}
incoming = [{to: "ada", id: 1}, {id: 2, to: "bo"}]
incoming | filter(r => not loaded.contains(fingerprint(r)[0:12]))
```

```text
[{id: 2, to: "bo"}]
```

Within one program, `distinct` already removes records equal under HQL
equality, and [[deduplication|Deduplication]] covers duplicates
by key. The fingerprint is for the comparison that crosses a run.

### A file's digest

`source(file(p), Text)` reads a file whole as one `String`, as
[[09-io/source|Source]] shows. Its digest is the one `shasum -a 256`
prints for the file, so a program checks a download against a published
digest:

```hql
import crypto.digest
expected = "ade391083e197c4575f5ccb436b35c499665e77202518e6873a02fa6b54cc8e3"
source(file("examples/io/counts.csv"), Text) | map(t => t.digest(Sha256) == expected)
```

```text
[true]
```

The reference states every function of the two modules with its signature:
[[crypto/digest|crypto.digest]] and
[[encoding]].
